/// Commits a SUCCESSFUL flush cycle: consumes the resolved ids and
/// swaps the pinned steering delivery to the one just written. The
/// flush calls this only after both pin appends succeeded.
void commitFlush() {
 _pinOnFlush.reset();
 _pinOnStering.reset();
 ...advance pinnedSteringRecordIds...
}
``

Wait, but commitFlush needs to know the steeringPin ids to advance `pinnedSteringRecordIds`. It can read `_pinOnStering.recordIds` before reset:

```dart
void commitFlush() {
 final steeringPin = _pinOnStering.recordIds;
 if (steringPin.isNotEmpty) {
   pinnedSteringRecordIds = List.of(steringPin);
 }
 _pinOnFlush.reset();
 _pinOnStering.reset();
}
``

And `_flushChildSteringPins`:

```dart
Future<void> _flushChildSteringPins(String id, Session session) async {
 final pins = _childSteringPins[id];
 if (pins == null) return;
 final (:release, :pin) = pins.takeForFlush();
 try {
   if (release.isNotEmpty) {
     await session.appendSegmentPin(recordIds: release, pinned: false);
   }
   if (pin.isNotEmpty) {
     await session.appendSegmentPin(recordIds: pin, pinned: true);
   }
 } on Object {
   // Best-effort by contract: a failed append leaves the resolved ids
   // pending — the next flush genuinely retries (commit-on-success;
   // gh-2492 review round 1). A failed flush must not affect the
   // child's result, and no bare print here: in TUI mode stdout IS the
   // terminal (gh-1510 precedent).
 }
 pins.commitFlush();
}
``

Hmm wait — commitFlush should be OUTSIDE the try? If append throws, commitFlush must NOT run. So:

```dart
 var committed = false;
 try {
   ...appends...
   committed = true;
 } on Object {
   ...
 }
 if (committed) pins.commitFlush();
``

Or put commitFlush inside the try after the appends (if commitFlush itself throws — it can't, pure memory ops — fine). Cleaner:

```dart
try {
 if (release.isNotEmpty) {
   await session.appendSegmentPin(recordIds: release, pinned: false);
 }
 if (pin.isNotEmpty) {
   await session.appendSegmentPin(recordIds: pin, pinned: true);
 }
 // Both appends landed: consume the resolved ids and swap the pinned
 // delivery (commit-on-success — a failed append leaves everything
 // pending, the next flush retries).
 pins.commitFlush();
} on Object {
 // Best-effort by contract (gh-2492 review round 1): a failed append
 // leaves the resolved ids PENDING — the next flush genuinely retries,
 // no pin is silently lost and no bogus release is queued. Silent like
 // the transcript catch above: in TUI mode stdout IS the terminal
 // (gh-1510) and a failed flush must not affect the child's result.
}
``

Wait, subtle issue: partial success — the release append succeeds but the pin append throws. Then commit doesn't run → the release ids stay "pending release"? Next flush: takeForFlush returns release: previous pinnedSteringRecordIds AGAIN (unchanged since commit didn't advance) — wait no: release in takeForFlush returns `pinnedSteringRecordIds` (the OLD delivery) when steeringPin non-empty. Next flush re-emits the same release (appendSegmentPin pinned:false for the old delivery AGAIN) — re-releasing is idempotent-ish (unpin again = same state) and then re-pins everything. That's safe: last-write-wins per id, release-then-pin adjacency preserved. The file gets a duplicate release record — harmless (same ids, same pinned:false). Good, honest retry semantics.

Also: what if release succeeded and pin failed, and next cycle has NO new steering pins (steringPin empty)? Then takeForFlush returns (release: [], pin: promptPin...) — wait: "A release rides a new steering pin only — a standalone release would unpin the newest steering with nothing replacing it" — hmm, with commit-on-success, if the pin failed, the OLD delivery's release was ALREADY written to the file! That unpins the old delivery with nothing replacing it — the exact hazard? Wait no — the old release record unpins old delivery, and the pin for the new delivery failed. The old steering is now UNPINED in the file. The pending new steering pin remains pending and will pin on next flush. Between those flushes, the old steering is unpinned — it could be hidden by a compaction pass in that window. Hmm.

But wait — was that any different BEFORE my change? Before: takeForFlush advanced pinnedSteringRecordIds immediately; release written; pin write throws → resolved ids consumed. So old delivery released (unpinned in file), new delivery never pins, AND next flush emits release for ids never pinned. Worse.

With commit-on-success: on FULL failure (release itself failed), nothing written, full retry. On partial failure (release landed, pin failed): old released, new pending. Next flush: steeringPin non-empty (the failed pin's ids are still pending) → release = pinnedSteringRecordIds (old delivery ids) → ANOTHER release for old ids (redundant but harmless) → then pin new. So the window where old is unpinned persists until next flush, but the new delivery gets pinned. This matches "best-effort" with a genuinely-retrying contract. The reviewer's suggested semantics: "copy the resolved lists, run the appends, and only reset the lists / advance pinnedSteringRecordIds after both appends succeed; on failure leave everything pending" — exactly what I implement. The partial-failure re-release is an acceptable consequence (unpin-then-pin ordering preserved; the reviewer's own suggestion has this property).

Hmm — one more consideration: should the release re-emit on retry? If release append failed AND pin append failed, next flush re-emits both — correct. If release succeeded, pin failed, next flush re-emits release for OLD delivery (already unpinned — writing pinned:false again is a no-op state-wise). Fine.

Now — is `_ChildSteringPinState` private to task_executor.dart (part of the same file, line 2590)? Yes, same file. Tests can't touch it directly; tests exercise via TaskExecutor with a session factory whose session throws. Let me look at the existing test subagent_stering_pins_test.dart to see fixtures.

Let me read the existing tests now. First ledger_budget_test.dart (12 tests per PR description), hide_batch_labels_test.dart, ledger_budget_projection_test.dart, subagent_stering_pins_test.dart.

